Sphincter of Oddi Dysfunction (Functional Biliary Pain)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Recurrent severe right upper quadrant pain, Complete absence of gallstones/structural obstruction, Post-cholecystectomy persistence of pain, Elevated sphincter pressure on ERCP manometry

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-046
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, incapacitating right upper quadrant abdominal pain and forceful vomiting mimicking an acute biliary or pancreatic emergency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with severe, escalating right upper quadrant pain radiating to the back, accompanied by recurrent forceful vomiting. Urgent after-hours assessment was critically required to immediately perform an abdominal examination, definitively exclude an acute surgical abdomen, acute ascending cholangitis, or perforated viscus, and provide parenteral analgesia/anti-emetics prior to the next available in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My stomach is tearing apart right under my ribs and shooting into my back, I've thrown up five times since dinner and I can't stand the pain."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with a 4-hour severe exacerbation of recurrent, distinct, and severe episodes of acute biliary-type pain strictly localized to the right upper quadrant and epigastrium. The pain began acutely after consuming a heavy, fatty dinner and rapidly escalated, causing severe nausea and recurrent forceful vomiting. The pain radiates directly through to the right subscapular region. The patient reports a previous history of an uncomplicated cholecystectomy two years ago which entirely failed to resolve the presenting pain. The patient strictly denies high fevers, rigors, or recent jaundice.
Functional Status: Curled in a fetal position on the bathroom floor, unable to sit up or mobilize.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound distress, high risk of acute clinical dehydration due to intractable vomiting; living alone.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 148/92 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Diaphoretic, pale, actively retching into a basin, in visible agony.
Physical Exam: Abdominal examination reveals severe, exquisite tenderness in the right upper quadrant and epigastrium upon deep palpation. However, there is a complete absence of localized peritonism, true guarding, or board-like rigidity. Murphy's sign is negative (no gallbladder present). Sclerae are completely anicteric. Bowel sounds are active and normal. Documented history of a complete absence of retained common bile duct stones on recent ultrasound, alongside a formally documented elevated sphincter basal pressure exceeding 40 mmHg on ERCP biliary manometry.
Environmental Observations: Half-eaten high-fat takeaway meal on the kitchen counter.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted oral paracetamol, immediately vomited.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Sphincter of Oddi Dysfunction (Functional Biliary Pain) - Status: Severe acute spastic flare
Differentials:

Acute Ascending Cholangitis - Ruled out by the strictly normal temperature (no fever), completely normal sclerae (no jaundice), and soft abdomen.

Acute Pancreatitis - Unlikely given the specifically localized RUQ pain rather than severe central epigastric pain radiating directly to the mid-back, though requires routine lipase confirmation.

Peptic Ulcer Perforation - Ruled out explicitly by the absence of a rigid, board-like abdomen and lack of generalized peritonism.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to agonizing, incapacitating upper abdominal pain and intractable vomiting that clinically perfectly simulated a severe acute surgical emergency (e.g., retained CBD stone, acute cholangitis, or perforation). An immediate, hands-on clinical examination was absolutely mandatory to definitively rule out a rigid surgical abdomen and assess clinical hydration status. By confirming the absence of peritonism and utilizing the documented manometry history, the physician safely diagnosed a severe functional biliary spasm. Prompt parenteral administration of antispasmodics and anti-emetics rapidly aborted the severe pain cycle, directly preventing a highly distressed and unnecessary emergency department admission, satisfying the strict criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IM Ketorolac 30mg stat for potent non-opioid systemic analgesia (avoiding opioids which can actively exacerbate sphincter of Oddi spasm). Administered IM Ondansetron 4mg stat.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of eating three large, heavy meals a day, strictly transition to eating five very small, ultra-low-fat meals. By physically removing the massive lipid load that triggers the severe biliary spasm, the patient directly prevents the agonizing attacks from initiating, immediately rewarding adherence with a profound improvement in daily quality of life and reduced fear of eating.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized gastrointestinal dietitian.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Urgent GP follow-up in 3 days to arrange baseline liver function tests and lipase, and to consider calcium channel blockers for sphincter relaxation.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing a high systemic fever, shaking chills, sudden yellowing of the eyes or skin, or if the abdomen becomes hard as a board and agonizing to the lightest touch.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Acute management assumes previously clear structural imaging remains accurate; the development of jaundice or fever requires immediate surgical review.
Clinical Course: Sphincter of Oddi Dysfunction is a severe muscle cramp of the valve that releases bile. It is excruciatingly painful but mechanically benign. Because your gallbladder is gone, this valve is taking the full pressure of bile release and spasming under the strain of heavy meals.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the whites of your eyes turn yellow, or if your stomach becomes hard and severely painful to touch.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The valve at the end of your bile duct is currently in a severe, locked cramp, building up massive pressure that causes your pain. Strong opioid painkillers actually make this specific valve clamp down even harder, which is why we used a specific anti-inflammatory injection to break the spasm without worsening the lock.
Actionable Steps: For the next 48 hours, consume absolutely zero fat (clear broths, plain rice, apple juice). Taking the chemical workload entirely off the biliary system will allow the inflamed, spasming valve to physically relax and reset, directly breaking the severe pain cycle.

